        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Covers</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these covers. They were chosen from listings, maker information and
            independent roundups, all checked on 15 October 2026, and the claims on this page are
            limited to what those sources support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from the situations that change the answer — a folding treadmill stored
            upright, a non-folding machine, a garage or covered outdoor space, and a machine that
            does not match standard cover sizes — and looked for covers that are on sale now in the
            US. For each one, we compared the dimensions and fabric in the listing title, the bullet
            points and the specification table, and then against a second source: another retailer
            listing or an independent roundup that named the same product. One card is an exception:
            the made-to-measure cover we would have listed for odd-sized machines is not sold on
            Amazon, so the oversized Rilime cover stands in for it, and its card says we could not
            confirm its figures from a second source.
          </p>
          <p>
            That step removed more covers than it kept. We excluded the <strong>QWORK</strong> folding cover because its height appears as 66 inches in one place and 69 in another,
            and its specification table lists details that cannot apply to a cover. We excluded the <strong>folding version of the Zettum cover</strong> because its listing gives
            46 x 38 x 66 inches in the bullets and 79 x 38 x 60 inches in its own table. We left out
            an <strong>XYZCTEM</strong> vented cover because its bullets and table disagree on which
            dimension is the height, and we could not see a price. For <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pads</a>, the <strong>Aidetech</strong> cover lists Oxford cloth in its bullets and polypropylene in
            its table, and another under-desk cover named 420D fabric in its title and 210D in its
            description, so neither made the list. Model-specific covers whose listings gave
            placeholder dimensions were also excluded, as was a well-reviewed cover sold only in
            Australia.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also checked the CPSC recall database for every brand listed or excluded here and for
            treadmill covers generally, and found no recalls. Where a figure appears below, it is
            because two sources agreed on it. Where only one source gave it — the weight of a cover,
            say, or a custom maker's fabric weights — we have left it out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Measure Your Treadmill for a Cover</h2>
<p>
            Covers are sold by three numbers — length, width and height — and listings use the
            words inconsistently. Some say "D x W x H", some say "L x W x H", and at least one listing
            we looked at swapped length and height between its title and its table. Measure your own
            machine first, in the position it will be covered in, and compare shapes rather than
            trusting the labels.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Folding treadmills: measure it folded</h3>
          <p>
            If you fold the treadmill between sessions, the cover goes on in the folded position, so
            that is the shape to measure. Fold it exactly as the manual describes, make sure the
            latch or hydraulic lock is engaged, and take three measurements:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Height</strong> from the floor to the highest point, which is usually the
            folded deck or the top of the console.</li>
            <li><strong>Width</strong> at the widest point, which is often the handrails or the
            console rather than the base.</li>
            <li><strong>Depth</strong> from the front of the base to the furthest point behind the
            folded deck, including the rear feet or wheels.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A typical folded treadmill comes out tall and short — roughly the shape of the
            46 x 38 x 66-inch folding covers on this page. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill dimensions and space requirements</a> explains how folded and in-use footprints differ.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Non-folding treadmills: measure it as it stands</h3>
          <p>
            For a non-folding machine, measure the overall length including the motor hood and the
            rear roller housing, the width at the handrails, and the height to the top of the
            console. Those machines are long and low, which is the shape of the 79 x 38 x 60-inch
            covers here. If your treadmill is longer than about 79 inches or taller than about 60,
            a standard cover will not reach the floor, and a made-to-measure cover is the answer.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How much allowance to leave</h3>
          <p>
            Choose a cover a few inches larger than your measurements in each direction, so it goes
            on without straining at the seams and the hem reaches close to the floor. Much more than
            that is a problem rather than a safety margin: excess fabric bunches at the base, catches
            feet and gives wind something to grab. Elastic hems and drawstrings take up a little
            slack; they cannot make a cover a size smaller.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more measurement is worth taking: the gap between the machine and the nearest wall.
            A cover adds bulk, and a folded treadmill pushed tight into a corner may not leave room
            to pull one on. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-fold-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding a treadmill</a> covers doing that safely before you cover it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indoor-vs-weatherproof" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Indoor Dust Covers Versus Weatherproof Covers</h2>
<p>
            Almost every treadmill cover on sale is described as "waterproof", "dustproof" and "for
            indoor and outdoor use", so those words do not help you choose. What does help is the
            fabric weight and coating, and being honest about which job you need done.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Indoors</strong>, a cover is a dust cover. It keeps dust, pet hair and fibre off
            the belt and the deck edges, keeps sunlight off the console and belt if the machine sits
            near a window, and makes the machine a little less inviting to curious children and
            animals. Any of the covers on this page does that job. A lighter fabric is easier to
            take on and off every day, and that matters more than toughness, because a cover that
            is a nuisance to remove tends to stay in a heap on the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In a garage or a covered outdoor space</strong>, the cover also has to deal with
            moisture, temperature swings and, in an open carport or patio, wind and sun. That is
            where a heavier fabric earns its place. Denier — the "420D" or "600D" in a listing — is
            a measure of yarn thickness: a higher number generally means a heavier, more
            tear-resistant fabric, though weave and coating matter too. Polyurethane (PU) and PVC
            coatings are what make a cover shed water.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two cautions. First, no cover turns an indoor treadmill into an outdoor one. Most
            treadmill manuals specify indoor use, and many warranties exclude damage from use in
            other conditions; our page on <a href="/outdoor-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using a treadmill outdoors</a> covers what that means in practice. A cover on a patio reduces exposure; it does not
            make the electronics weatherproof. Second, "waterproof" and "breathable" pull in
            opposite directions, which is the subject of the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="condensation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Condensation, and Why Breathability Matters in a Garage</h2>
<p>
            In a garage, the biggest risk to a treadmill's electronics is not rain but condensation.
            When warm, humid air meets a cold surface, water forms on it. A garage that is cold
            overnight and warms quickly in the morning, or that has its door opened on a humid day,
            does exactly that to a cold treadmill frame, console and controller board.
          </p>
          <p>
            A cover can help or hurt. A fabric cover slows down how fast the air around the machine
            changes, which takes the edge off rapid swings. But a fully sealed, non-breathable cover
            traps whatever moisture is already under it, and when the temperature drops, that
            moisture condenses on the inside of the cover and on the machine. That is why our guides
            to the <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">garage</a> and to <a href="/how-to-store-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">storing a treadmill</a> both warn against plastic sheeting and tarpaulins.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most PU-coated covers, including three of the four here, sit somewhere in between: they
            are woven fabric with a water-resistant coating, so they shed drips but do not breathe
            the way an uncoated canvas or cotton dust sheet does. Some makers describe their coated
            fabric as breathable; treat that as a claim rather than a measurement. In practice, a
            few habits make a coated cover work in a garage:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Never cover a damp machine.</strong> Wipe off sweat and let the treadmill
            dry fully first, as our <a href="/how-to-clean-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cleaning guide</a> describes.</li>
            <li><strong>Leave the hem loose</strong> unless it is windy, so air can move under the
            cover rather than being sealed in.</li>
            <li><strong>Prefer vents.</strong> Covered air vents let moist air escape without
            letting rain in. Made-to-measure covers from makers such as Covers &amp; All, sold on
            their own sites rather than Amazon, include them as standard; several marketplace
            listings mention vents in their titles but not in their descriptions, so we have not
            credited them.</li>
            <li><strong>Take the cover off if you see moisture inside it</strong>, and let the
            machine and the cover dry before using either.</li>
            <li><strong>Deal with the damp, not just the machine.</strong> In a garage that is damp
            year-round, a dehumidifier and a mat between the machine and the concrete do more than
            any cover.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            And whatever the cover, never switch the treadmill on with visible moisture on the frame
            or console. Let it dry and warm with the room first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Covering Safely: Heat, Power, Pets and Children</h2>
<p>
            A cover is a sheet of fabric over an electrical machine, which means a few rules are not
            optional.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never cover a running treadmill, or one that has just been used.</strong> The
            motor and controller are cooled by air drawn through the <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>motor compartment</a>, and both
            stay warm for a while after a session. A cover thrown over a hot motor traps that heat.
            Switch the machine off, let it cool — longer after a long or hard session — and only
            then cover it. Our guide to <a href="/treadmill-overheating/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill overheating</a> explains why heat around the motor matters.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Unplug it and take the safety key out.</strong> A covered treadmill is not being
            watched. Unplugging protects it from surges while nobody is around and removes any
            chance of the belt starting. Keep the safety key somewhere separate, out of children's
            reach, not on the console under the cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the power cord clear.</strong> Do not tuck the cord under the cover's hem,
            pinch it under a drawstring, or route it where the cover drags on it. A cord trapped
            under fabric is a cord nobody inspects.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Children and pets.</strong> A cover makes a treadmill less of an attraction, but
            it is not a barrier, and a draped machine can become a den or a climbing frame. Do not
            let children play under or on a covered treadmill, make sure a folded machine's lock is
            engaged before the cover goes on, and keep pets away from the hem, where loose fabric and drawstrings
            can tangle. A drawstring with a free end is worth tucking away. Our <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a> cover the rest of what keeps children and animals safe around a treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Never use the cover while the belt is moving.</strong> Take it fully off, fold
            it, and put it away from the machine before you start. A cover left on the frame or
            draped over a handrail can slide onto the belt and be pulled toward the rollers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fitting-and-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Putting a Cover On, and Looking After It</h2>
<p>
            Most covers go on from the top down, with the elastic hem or drawstring at the base.
            On a folding treadmill, fold and lock the machine first, then find the cover's front —
            some have a label or a handle — and pull it down evenly on all sides. On a non-folding
            machine, start at the console end and work back toward the rear roller, so the cover
            does not snag on the handrails.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use the drawstring for wind, not by default.</strong> In an open carport or on a
            patio, a drawstring or buckle keeps the cover from blowing off. Indoors and in a closed
            garage, a looser hem lets the machine breathe.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the cover clean.</strong> Dust on the outside of a cover ends up on the
            machine when you take it off. Shake it outside occasionally, and wash or rinse it as the
            maker describes; several listings say hand wash or rinse only. Let it dry fully before
            it goes back on.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Store it properly.</strong> Some covers, including our folding pick, come with a storage bag. A
            folded cover kept off the floor is less likely to collect dust, damp and the attention
            of pets between uses.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pair it with a mat.</strong> A cover protects the top; a mat protects the
            bottom, keeping the frame off cold, damp concrete and stopping dust being drawn into the
            motor compartment from floor level. Our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill mat roundup</a> covers sizes for each kind of machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Walking Pads and Under-Desk Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            Walking pads need a different shape of cover altogether: long, narrow and only a few
            inches tall. Many are sold, usually in a dozen or more sizes under one listing, and that
            is part of the problem. The two walking-pad covers we looked at most closely both
            contradicted themselves on fabric, one naming Oxford cloth in its description and
            polypropylene in its table, and another naming 420D fabric in its title and 210D in its
            bullets. We have not listed one for that reason.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a walking-pad cover, measure the pad's length, width and height, including
            any fold-down handrail, and choose a listing that gives the exact size you need rather
            than a "universal" one. Check that the fabric is named the same way throughout the
            listing. And consider whether you need one at all: a walking pad slid under a bed or a
            sofa, or stood on its end in a cupboard, is already protected from most dust. If it lives
            under a desk, an elastic-hem cover is mainly about keeping pet hair out of the belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Does Not Need a Treadmill Cover</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If the treadmill is used daily in a clean indoor room</strong>, a cover adds a
            chore for little benefit. A quick wipe after each session and a weekly vacuum around
            the machine do more for it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the machine is going into long-term storage</strong>, a cover is part of the
            job but not the main part. Cleaning, drying, <a href="/how-tight-should-treadmill-belt-be/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>belt tension</a>, the plug and the room matter
            more, and our guide to <a href="/how-to-store-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">storing a treadmill</a> covers them in order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the problem is a cold or damp garage</strong>, a cover will not solve it on
            its own. A machine built to cope with those conditions makes more difference, and our
            guide to the <a href="/best-treadmills-for-garage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for a garage</a> looks at which machines suit them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If the treadmill sits fully outdoors</strong>, uncovered by a roof, no cover on
            this page is a substitute for moving it indoors. The weatherproof picks reduce exposure
            in a carport or under a patio roof; they are not designed for a machine left in the open.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes With Treadmill Covers</h2>
          <p>Each of these turns a simple protective cover into something that does no good, or does harm.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring a folding treadmill unfolded</h3>
              <p>A folding treadmill is covered in its folded position, which is tall and short, not long and low. Measured unfolded, you will buy a non-folding cover that bunches on the floor. Fold and lock the machine, then measure the height, the widest width and the depth, and compare those to the cover listing, not the belt size on the box.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Covering a warm or sweaty machine</h3>
              <p>A cover over a motor that has just been working traps heat, and a cover over a sweaty frame traps moisture. Switch off, unplug, remove the safety key, wipe the rails and belt, and let the machine cool and dry fully before the cover goes on. Never put a cover on a running treadmill for any reason.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Sealing a coated cover tight in a damp garage</h3>
              <p>Most covers are coated to shed water, which also means they breathe less. Cinched tight in a garage that cools at night, they can hold moisture against the machine. Leave the hem loose unless wind is a problem, choose a vented cover where you can, take it off if you see moisture inside, and tackle the damp itself with a dehumidifier.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the listing title alone</h3>
              <p>Marketplace cover listings often give one size in the title and another in the table, or name different fabrics in different places. Read the title, the bullet points and the specification table, check they agree, and confirm the style option selected. If they disagree, ask the seller or buy a cover whose listing is consistent.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Measure first, then buy the simplest cover that fits.</strong> For a folding treadmill, a 46 x 38 x 66-inch cover such as the ClawsCover folding model
            suits most machines; for a non-folding one, a 79 x 38 x 60-inch cover. Choose the heavier
            Zettum fabric for a carport or open garage, the larger Rilime cover for a long or tall
            machine, and a made-to-measure vented cover, bought from its maker, when your machine is an
            odd size or condensation is a real problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whatever you buy, only cover a cool, dry, unplugged treadmill, leave the hem loose
            indoors, and never use a plastic sheet in a garage. If the machine lives out there, our
            guide to <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">keeping a treadmill in a garage</a> covers the rest of the set-up.
          </p>
        </div>

