        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Should I put a cover on my treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If the treadmill sits in a garage, a dusty room, near a window, or goes unused for weeks, a cover is worth having: it keeps dust off the belt, sun off the console and some damp off the frame. If you use the machine daily in a clean indoor room, wiping it after each session and vacuuming around it matters more than a cover.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What size treadmill cover do I need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Measure the machine in the position you will cover it. Fold a folding treadmill and measure height, widest width and depth; measure a non-folding machine as it stands, including the motor hood and rear roller housing. Choose a cover a few inches larger in each direction. Folding machines commonly suit about 46 x 38 x 66 inches; non-folding about 79 x 38 x 60.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a waterproof treadmill cover good for a garage?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, used carefully. A coated cover sheds drips and slows temperature swings, but it breathes less than plain fabric, so moisture already under it can condense when the garage cools. Only cover a dry machine, leave the hem loose unless it is windy, prefer a cover with vents, and run a dehumidifier if the garage is damp.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I leave a treadmill outside under a cover?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not as a long-term plan. Most treadmill manuals specify indoor use, and environmental damage may not be covered by the warranty. A heavy, coated cover reduces exposure under a carport or patio roof, but it does not make the motor, controller or console weatherproof. If the machine has to live outdoors, choose one built for it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I cover my treadmill right after using it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The motor and controller are still warm after a session, and a cover traps that heat and any sweat on the frame. Switch the treadmill off, unplug it, take the safety key out, wipe off sweat, and let the machine cool and dry before covering it. Never cover a treadmill that is running.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a plastic sheet or tarp OK as a treadmill cover?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is the worst choice in a garage or basement. Plastic sheeting and tarpaulins trap humid air against the machine, and when the temperature drops, water condenses on the inside and drips onto the frame and console. A fabric treadmill cover, or a cotton or canvas dust sheet, is better. If plastic is all you have, leave the bottom open.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

